  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{9}{(y-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{9}{(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{x}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>2,$ we have\n\n$$\n|y-2|=y-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}+2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
